rence/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "q-326334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a single branch, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/b8b14a6d9bf2f77c79b26172ec18e7833c1315ccb09c94be5a05b7e12714a14f.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $(-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}),$ we get only the upper branch.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe only the upper branch are\n\n$$\nx=4\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=5\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "q-326334" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "329d1f0065d3888ba585f3ccecb0bb41ca9da343cb5d327e6a2f72c539991173"} {"question" "q-326334" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "q-326334" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "q-326334" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "q-326334" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{\\pi}{2}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "q-91323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/7be6e3cc933586287cabf590d2a3c5fede604b6c4222a39c9db78f3a3745f814.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "q-91323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,\\pi ],\\,t\\ne \\pm \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ebb0f765fca0bc4e3a0a789800779a51eb4173cf0384b8b3628ac679a5a3d692.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\operatorname{tan}⁡t,\\,y=3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t,\\,t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2}).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "q-91323" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "329d1f0065d3888ba585f3ccecb0bb41ca9da343cb5d327e6a2f72c539991173"} {"question" "q-91323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "q-91323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},0]"] ["math" "t\\in (-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "t\\in (\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\pi ]"] ["math" "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] ["math" "t\\in [0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "q-91323" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "t\\in [-\\pi ,-\\frac{\\pi }{2})"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/state.json" "file_sha256" "c64e8f0af83179155888a6b930b1d68726436c63c175453879edc323b0dc8b16"} {"question" "e-7886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{y^2}{9} - \\dfrac{x^2}{25} = 1?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/example-7886.json" "file_sha256" "f92677a0a0016bded62408b19f8eab8a12412fe60d5cf0cbc017762668adb92e"} {"question" "e-7886" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx = b\\tan{t}, \\quad y = a\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $a = \\sqrt{9} = 3$ and $b = \\sqrt{25} = 5.$ Therefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx = 5\\tan{t}, \\quad y = 3\\sec{t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/example-7886.json" "file_sha256" "f92677a0a0016bded62408b19f8eab8a12412fe60d5cf0cbc017762668adb92e"} {"question" "e-7884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/ac922b185c42645f703c13d4f5016941b4125d81e85fa5ff529aa5c8d4010ac8.png)\n\nFind the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/example-7884.json" "file_sha256" "27b57f64e4645936637cb8261d8e1662ce85e21006d875576e37e849771f6dbe"} {"question" "e-7884" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by \n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t},\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0, \\pm a).$ From the graph, we have that $(0,9)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=9.$\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola are $y = \\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}x.$ In our case, the asymptotes are $y = \\pm 3 x,$ and so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{a}{b} & =3 \\\\ \\frac{9}{b} & =3 \\\\ b & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\tan{t},\\quad y=9\\sec{t}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/example-7884.json" "file_sha256" "27b57f64e4645936637cb8261d8e1662ce85e21006d875576e37e849771f6dbe"} {"question" "e-7887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x =2\\tan{t}, \\, y =3\\sec{t}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/example-7887.json" "file_sha256" "7eeaf8b671906faf2d51c05a744d18040114326ad3e8e8508396f898b988952b"} {"question" "e-7887" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t.$ We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{x}{2} & =\\operatorname{tan}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{{2}^{2}} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & ={tan}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =3\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{y}{3} & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{{3}^{2}} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} & ={sec}^{2}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity $\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}$ and rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{tan}^{2}⁡t+1 & ={sec}^{2}⁡t \\\\ \\frac{{x}^{2}}{4}+1 & =\\frac{{y}^{2}}{9} \\\\ \\frac{{y}^{2}}{9}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/example-7887.json" "file_sha256" "7eeaf8b671906faf2d51c05a744d18040114326ad3e8e8508396f898b988952b"} {"question" "e-8055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/2944e095d71b64fb2f9e6e0121a09fb9eb2f3ffe765f9881162c95fe6875a831.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}.\n$$\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/example-8055.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c285b1a924a557f05c895f4b5ce78c6e6ecf5cea48b7093f580de47866456"} {"question" "e-8055" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad\nt \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad\nt \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2.\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/assets/b1d78a939f85c533bcd9b4609346f2a15385aad3a6b074a62b791551cf784418.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad\ny = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad\nt \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,0\\right].\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14062908/example-8055.json" "file_sha256" "bd5c285b1a924a557f05c895f4b5ce78c6e6ecf5cea48b7093f580de47866456"}] "usage" {"q-91323" [] "q-89068" [] "q-90951" [] "e-8055" [] "e-7887" [] "e-7886" [] "e-7884" []} "basis" 2058 "retractions" [[17592186157905 :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\frac{y^{2}}{25} - \\frac{x^{2}}{16} = 1$?"] [17592186157935 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/q-90951.png)\n\nOne branch of a hyperbola (shown above) is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\tan t, y = 2\\sec t$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?"] [17592186157936 :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/q-91323.png)\n\nThe quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$x = 3\\tan t, y = 3\\sec t$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?"] [17592186157107 :question/problem "What are the parametric equations of the hyperbola $\\dfrac{y^2}{9} - \\dfrac{x^2}{25} = 1$?"] [17592186157107 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{y^2}{a^2} - \\frac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx = b\\tan{t}, \\quad y = a\\sec{t}\n$$\n\nIn our case,\n\n$$\na = \\sqrt{9} = 3\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\nb = \\sqrt{25} = 5\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations are\n\n$$\nx = 5\\tan{t}, \\quad y = 3\\sec{t}\n$$"] [17592186157106 :question/problem "Find the parametric equations of the hyperbola shown above given that its asymptotes are $y=\\pm 3x$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/8c6c7cf5762a3078063987d1dc5eb95f.png)"] [17592186157106 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of a vertical hyperbola centered at the origin with Cartesian equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y^2}{a^2} - \\dfrac{x^2}{b^2} = 1\n$$\n\nare given by\n\n$$\nx=b\\tan{t},\\quad y=a\\sec{t}\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are positive constants.\n\nThe vertices of the hyperbola occur at $(0, \\pm a)$. From the graph, we have that $(0,9)$ is a vertex of the hyperbola, so $a=9$.\n\nThe asymptotes of a vertical hyperbola are\n\n$$\ny = \\pm \\dfrac{a}{b}x\n$$\n\nIn our case, the asymptotes are\n\n$$\ny = \\pm 3 x\n$$\n\nand so we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\frac{a}{b} &= 3 \\\\\n\\frac{9}{b} &= 3 \\\\\nb &= 3\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx=3\\tan{t},\\quad y=9\\sec{t}\n$$"] [17592186157108 :question/problem "Find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola $x =2\\tan{t}, \\, y =3\\sec{t}$."] [17592186157108 :question/worked-solution "To find the Cartesian equation of the hyperbola, we need to eliminate the parameter $t$. We can do this using the secant-tangent Identity.\n\n- Isolating the $\\tan{t}$ term in the $x$-equation and squaring, we get\n$x|= 2\\tan t; \\frac{x}{2}|= \\tan t; \\frac{x^{2}}{2^{2}}|= \\tan^{2} t; \\frac{x^{2}}{4}|= \\tan^{2} t$.\n- Isolating the $\\sec{t}$ term in the $y$-equation and squaring, we get\n$y|= 3\\sec t; \\frac{y}{3}|= \\sec t; \\frac{y^{2}}{3^{2}}|= \\sec^{2} t; \\frac{y^{2}}{9}|= \\sec^{2} t$.\n\nSubstituting the two equations into the identity\n\n$$\n\\tan^2{t} + 1 = \\sec^2{t}\n$$\n\nand rearranging, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\tan^{2} t + 1 &= \\sec^{2} t \\\\\n\\frac{x^{2}}{4} + 1 &= \\frac{y^{2}}{9} \\\\\n\\frac{y^{2}}{9} - \\frac{x^{2}}{4} &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186157124 :question/problem "The quarter-hyperbola above is defined by the parametric equations\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/b31abfb21d7c23e3c4f5c2a1ed1eb344.png)\n\n$x = \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\qquad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}$.\n\nWhat is the domain of the parameter $t$?"] [17592186157124 :question/worked-solution "The parametric equations of the full hyperbola are\n\n$$\nx= \\sqrt3 \\tan{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt2 \\sec{t}, \\quad t \\in [-\\pi,\\pi], \\quad t \\neq \\pm\\dfrac{\\pi}2\n$$\n\nTo restrict the parameterization to a quarter-hyperbola, we refer to the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/2981/Source/Images/ff70cc02658a737d406109329d5c2ccf.png)\n\nWe can see that if we restrict $t$ to $[MATH: \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}, 0\\right]]$ we get the required quarter-hyperbola.\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations that describe the required quarter-hyperbola are\n\n$[MATH: x= \\sqrt3\\tan{t}, \\quad y = \\sqrt2\\sec{t}, \\quad t \\in \\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}2,0\\right].]$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186156683 17592186156684 17592186156685 17592186156686 17592186156687 17592186156863 17592186156864 17592186156865 17592186156866 17592186156867 17592186156869 17592186156870 17592186156871 17592186156872 17592186156873] "immutable_fields" [[17592186156682 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156682 :answer-field/id] [17592186156682 :answer-field/key] [17592186156682 :answer-field/type] [17592186156862 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156862 :answer-field/id] [17592186156862 :answer-field/key] [17592186156862 :answer-field/type] [17592186156868 :answer-field/correct] [17592186156868 :answer-field/id] [17592186156868 :answer-field/key] [17592186156868 :answer-field/type]] "no_history" []}
